
Sentiment Analysis with Spark NLP vs Hugging Face
in PySpark
1. Spark NLP Approach
Integration: It is developed natively on top of Apache Spark using native
Spark ML Pipelines. Workflow:
1. Raw text is structured using DocumentAssembler.
2. UniversalSentenceEncoder for dense embeddings with pretraining.
3. A classification model called SentimentDLModel was developed using
data from Twitter.
Benefits include full distribution, little bridging overhead, and spark
optimisation. Limitations: There are fewer models and fewer fine-tuning
choices.
2. Hugging Face Transformers in PySpark
Integration: Makes use of distributed inference or Pandas UDFs to
use Hugging Face models in Spark.
1.The first step in the workflow is to load a pretrained transformer
(such as distilbert-base-uncased-finetuned-sst-2-english).
2. Wrap inference in UDFs for Pandas.
3. Create a Spark DataFrame from the results.
Benefits include modular architectures, support for fine-tuning, and
access to hundreds of models. Limitations: Requires more memory
and engineering work.
3. Choosing Between Them
Use Spark NLP for fast deployment, pretrained models, and seamless scaling in
Spark.
Use Hugging Face when model variety, cutting-edge architectures, or
custom fine-tuning are priorities.
Feature Spark NLP Hugging Face in Spark
Model Variety Limited to Spark NLP repo Thousands of models available
Scaling Native distributed execution Needs Pandas UDF/batching
Fine-tuning Support Minimal Extensive (PyTorch/TF)
Ease of Setup Simple in Spark pipeline More engineering required
